          {
            title: 'Sink-Sink',
            subtitle: 'Card Game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Sink-Sink is a card game commonly played in Niger; a variant of Agram with five cards per player instead of six, dealt in sets of three and two. Players follow suit; the winner of each trick leads to the next. The player who wins the fifth and last trick wins the game.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Country/Region of Origin: Niger\nTime Period: Early 1900s\nHistorical Development: The game evolved from traditional card games in the region\nCultural Significance: Popular social and family game',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Minimum Players: 2\nMaximum Players: 6\nOptimal Players: 4\nPartnerships: None',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Type: 35 cards from standard 52-card pack (Kings, Queens, Jacks, Twos and Ace of Spades removed)\nNumber of Decks: 1\nJokers: Not included',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '35-card stripped deck (French suits)\nTable space',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Cards per Player: 5\nDealing Pattern: Three cards each, then two cards each (counter-clockwise from dealer)\nFirst dealer: any player by agreement; thereafter winner of previous game deals next',
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
